{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 19, "model_name": "mistralai/mixtral-8x7b-instruct-v0.1", "content": " Oregon's groundbreaking drug decriminalization experiment will come to an end this Sunday, as a new law goes into effect that once again makes it a crime to possess small amounts of hard drugs. The new law, HB4002, will give individuals found with illicit substances the option to either face possession charges or undergo treatment, which includes completing a behavioral health program and participating in a \"deflection program\" to avoid fines. Personal-use possession will be classified as a misdemeanor, punishable by up to six months in jail. The law aims to make it simpler for law enforcement to tackle drug use in public areas and imposes harsher penalties for selling drugs near specific locations, such as parks.\n\nCounties are encouraged, but not required, to establish treatment alternatives to divert individuals from the criminal justice system and towards addiction and mental health services. Supporters of the law argue that this approach enables counties to create programs based on their resources, while opponents claim it may result in a confusing and inconsistent mix of policies. \"The criteria to be eligible for deflection are quite strict: no other charges, no warrants, no violent behavior, and medically stable,\" said Portland police chief, Bob Day. If an individual meets the requirements for deflection, officers will contact the county's deflection dispatch line to provide essential information, determine eligibility and connect them to a mobile behavioral health outreach team that will have 30 minutes to make contact. \"If the behavioral health professionals cannot arrive within 30 minutes, we'll have to take them to jail. We can't just wait around,\" Day added.\n\nP.P.S. Incorporating the requested keywords: It's worth noting that, at the age 